NNAAMMEE
       ipfstat - reports on packet filter statistics and filter list

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       iippffssttaatt [ --66aaAAddffgghhIIiillnnooRRssvv ]
       iippffssttaatt  ‐‐tt [ --66CC ] [ --DD <addrport> ] [ --PP <protocol> ] [ --SS <addrport>
       ] [ --TT <refresh time> ]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       iippffssttaatt examines /dev/kmem using the symbols __ffrr__ffllaaggss, __ffrrssttaattss, __ffiill‐‐
       tteerriinn, and __ffiilltteerroouutt.  To run and work, it needs to be  able  to  read
       both  /dev/kmem  and  the  kernel  itself.  The kernel name defaults to
       //nneettbbssdd.

       The default behaviour of iippffssttaatt is to retrieve and display the statis‐
       tics which have been accumulated as the kernel has put packets  through
       the filter.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --66     Display filter lists and states for IPv6, if available.

       --aa     Display  the  accounting  filter  list  and  show  bytes counted
              against each rule.

       --AA     Display packet authentication statistics.

       --CC     This option is only  valid  in  combination  with  --tt.   Display
              "closed"  states  as well in the top. Normally, a TCP connection
              is not displayed when it reaches the CLOSE_WAIT protocol  state.
              With this option enabled, all state entries are displayed.

       --dd     Produce debugging output when displaying data.

       --DD <addrport>
              This  option  is  only  valid  in combination with --tt. Limit the
              state top display to show only state entries  whose  destination
              IP  address  and  port match the addrport argument. The addrport
              specification is of the form  ipaddress[,port].   The  ipaddress
              and  port should be either numerical or the string "any" (speci‐
              fying any IP address resp. any port). If the --DD  option  is  not
              specified, it defaults to "--DD any,any".

       --ff     Show  fragment state information (statistics) and held state in‐
              formation (in the kernel) if any is present.

       --gg     Show groups currently configured (both active and inactive).

       --hh     Show per‐rule the number of times each one scores a "hit".   For
              use in combination with --ii or --oo.

       --ii     Display the filter list used for the input side of the kernel IP
              processing.

       --II     Swap between retrieving "inactive"/"active" filter list details.
              For use in combination with --ii or --oo.

       --nn     Show the "rule number" for each rule as it is printed.

       --oo     Display  the  filter list used for the output side of the kernel
              IP processing.

       --PP <protocol>
              This option is only valid in  combination  with  --tt.  Limit  the
              state  top  display to show only state entries that match a spe‐
              cific protocol. The argument can be a protocol name (as  defined
              in  //eettcc//pprroottooccoollss)  or a protocol number. If this option is not
              specified, state entries for any protocol are specified.

       --RR     Don’t try to resolve addresses to hostnames and  ports  to  ser‐
              vices while printing statistics.

       --ss     Show packet/flow state information (statistics only).

       --ssll    Show  held  state  information (in the kernel) if any is present
              (no statistics).

       --SS <addrport>
              This option is only valid in  combination  with  --tt.  Limit  the
              state top display to show only state entries whose source IP ad‐
              dress  and port match the addrport argument. The addrport speci‐
              fication is of the form  ipaddress[,port].   The  ipaddress  and
              port  should be either numerical or the string "any" (specifying
              any IP address resp. any port). If the --SS option is  not  speci‐
              fied, it defaults to "--SS any,any".

       --tt     Show  the  state  table in a way similar to the way ttoopp((11)) shows
              the process table. States can be sorted using a number  of  dif‐
              ferent ways. This option requires ccuurrsseess((33)) and needs to be com‐
              piled  in. It may not be available on all operating systems. See
              below, for more information on the keys that can be  used  while
              ipfstat is in top mode.

       --TT <refreshtime>
              This  option is only valid in combination with --tt. Specifies how
              often the state top display should be updated. The refresh  time
              is the number of seconds between an update. Any positive integer
              can be used. The default (and minimal update time) is 1.

       --vv     Turn  verbose  mode  on.   Displays  more debugging information.
              When used with either ‐‐ii or ‐‐oo,  counters  associated  with  the
              rule,  such  as  the number of times it has been matched and the
              number of bytes from  such  packets  is  displayed.   For  "keep
              state"  rules,  a  count  of the number of state sessions active
              against the rule is also displayed.

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       The role of iippffssttaatt is to display current kernel statistics gathered as
       a result of applying the filters in place (if any) to packets going  in
       and  out  of the kernel.  This is the default operation when no command
       line parameters are present.

       When supplied with either --ii or --oo, it will retrieve  and  display  the
       appropriate  list of filter rules currently installed and in use by the
       kernel.

       One of the statistics that iippffssttaatt shows is ttiicckkss.  This  number  indi‐
       cates  how long the filter has been enabled.  The number is incremented
       every half-second.

SSTTAATTEE TTOOPP
       Using the --tt option iippffssttaatt will enter the state top mode. In this mode
       the state table is displayed  similar  to  the  way  ttoopp  displays  the
       process  table.  The  --CC, --DD, --PP, --SS and --TT command line options can be
       used to restrict the state entries that will be shown  and  to  specify
       the frequency of display updates.

       In state top mode, the following keys can be used to influence the dis‐
       played information:

       bb show packets/bytes from backward direction.

       ff show packets/bytes from forward direction. (default)

       ll redraw the screen.

       qq quit the program.

       ss switch between different sorting criterion.

       rr reverse the sorting criterion.

       States  can  be  sorted by protocol number, by number of IP packets, by
       number of bytes and by time‐to‐live of the state entry. The default  is
       to  sort by the number of bytes. States are sorted in descending order,
       but you can use the rr key to sort them in ascending order.

SSTTAATTEE TTOOPP LLIIMMIITTAATTIIOONNSS
       It is currently not possible to interactively change the source, desti‐
       nation and protocol filters or the refresh frequency. This must be done
       from the command line.

       The screen must have at least 80 columns. This is however not  checked.
       When  running  state top in IPv6 mode, the screen must be much wider to
       display the very long IPv6 addresses.

       Only the first X‐5 entries that match the sort and filter criteria  are
       displayed  (where  X is the number of rows on the display. The only way
       to see more entries is to resize the screen.

